WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:13.540
Magnifying lenses like the ones in today’s
microscopes gave us, of course, our first

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.119
glimpses into the world of the microcosmos.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:22.540
But it was not the first time people had guessed
that there might be more to the world than

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:27.690
met our eyes, particularly regarding illness.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:34.620
Marcus Terentius Varro, a Roman scholar in
30 BC, wrote about the need to be cautious

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:43.330
around swamps “because there bred certain
animalculae which cannot be seen by the eyes,

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:49.580
which float in the air and enter the body
through the mouth and nose and there cause

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.150
serious diseases.”

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:58.160
Marcus, thus, managed to guess at a reality
that it would take thousands more years, and

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.470
a great deal of debate to confirm.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:08.750
Even now, when it comes to human understanding
of our unseen neighbors, our focus is almost

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.760
always driven by negative interactions.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:19.140
Our most vivid, collective encounters are
also some of the most unpleasant: the sneezing,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.540
the coughing, the upset stomachs, the death

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.920
evidence of an apparent tension between our bodies

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.380
and the microbial world.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:36.500
This strange little guy is a parasitic flatworm,
but for now, it’s in its cercarial form,

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.930
a larva in search of a host—preferably animals
like duck, geese, and raccoons—where it

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:49.320
can feed on their blood and then mature into
adulthood and release eggs through the infected

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.220
animal’s waste.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.050
From the eggs will emerge a ciliated larva
that will seek out particular species of aquatic

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:02.800
snails to serve as an intermediate host as
they develop into their cercarial stage and

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.740
complete the cycle.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:12.260
For all the hosts this parasite will go through,
it turns out that we humans are not a particularly

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.939
great one.They can infect us, and when they
do, they will burrow into the skin and create

00:02:18.939 --> 00:02:26.359
little bumps, inciting an allergic reaction
that produces a rash formally known as cercarial

00:02:26.359 --> 00:02:31.489
dermatitis, but it’s more frequently known
as “swimmer’s itch” thanks to its aquatic

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:32.930
origin.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.569
But for the cercaria, the real challenge is
that we just aren’t the right bodies to

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:42.920
house its development into adulthood, and
instead, it will die.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:53.060
The word “germs” might be the very first term
many of us learn to describe microbes, an

00:02:53.069 --> 00:03:00.469
easy word that warns us from an early age
that there are things we cannot see that nonetheless

00:03:00.469 --> 00:03:02.510
we must watch out for.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.739
And with medical necessity often shaping the
direction of scientific research, their potential

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:15.159
danger is further emphasized because it has
inspired and shaped so much of our understanding

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:17.209
of microorganisms.

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:24.550
And so we know a great deal about the microbes
that can hurt us and next to nothing about

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.930
the ones that can’t.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:33.430
But necessity is now driving us to better
grasp the complexity of microbes in relation

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:40.389
to us, the idea that they are only ever enemies
is contradicted by our developing understanding

00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:47.650
of the organisms that live in and on us, not
just as freeloaders, but potentially as part

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:50.349
of our body’s mechanics.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:56.569
Words like “microbiome” and “probiotic”
are now part of our understanding of the world,

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:03.189
and also part of active research, though a
far smaller part than research focused on pathogens.

00:04:03.189 --> 00:04:10.079
But, and stop me if you’ve heard this before,
it turns out, the world is bigger than humans,

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:17.590
and beyond our own perimeters, there’s the
importance of microbes to the world around us.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:23.920
What is known about that world is a tiny percentage
of what there is to know.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.200
Now as we delve more into the balance between
microbes that both help and harm, we do want

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.360
to remind you that however harmless or beautiful
or fascinating the microbes we show you here

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.030
are, you should still wash your hands and
get your shots, and having done away with

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:48.340
that though, let’s remind you, most microbes
cannot hurt you.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.440
While it would be challenging to calculate
the exact percentage of microbial species

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:00.020
out there that are pathogenic and could potentially
make us sick, estimates put it somewhere less

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.910
than 1%.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:07.040
Figuring out whether the bacteria in our samples
lie in that slim percentage would require

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:13.090
isolating and growing them up in cultures
so that we can sequence and properly identify them.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:17.980
But we just take the safe route and you know,
we don’t drink the pond water.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:25.780
Bacterial species do run a range as well,
giving us everything from cheese to tetanus.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:31.560
For us though, their presence helps sustain
the many bacteria-eaters we feature, and we

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:40.060
keep them fed on droplets of milk, water from
boiled hay, cooked rice, or even liquid plant fertilizer.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:45.740
Now Nematodes, on the other hand, might seem
like a more clear cut danger.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:50.730
More commonly known as roundworms, they are
plentiful, with estimates counting them at

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:59.800
around 60 billion nematodes for every person
on Earth, and around 40,000 species in total.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:06.210
But it’s a select few of those species that
are particularly notorious for humans, able

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:12.310
to enter humans as eggs in contaminated food
or as larva that can penetrate the skin depending

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:13.580
on the species.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:19.370
So it’s understandable if you respond to
the image of a nematode with some revulsion.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:25.750
But the vastness of them means that their
impact is so much more complex when you examine

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:30.610
them across their different intersections
with life, from ecosystems where they can

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:37.000
move other microbes around the soil and cycle
nutrients like nitrogen, to agriculture, where

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:43.830
some nematode species may be pests while others
are helpful controls of insect populations.

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:47.590
But these complications aren’t always strictly
about pathogens.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:54.590
We’ve featured Euglena here before, their twisty
bodies both fascinating and seemingly harmless.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:07:01.920
But in 2002, 21,000 striped bass died in a
facility in North Carolina.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.200
The culprit?

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:09.270
A bloom of Euglena sanguinea, which released
a toxin called euglenophycin that is similar

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.180
in structure to fire ant venom.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:19.590
And yet this danger poses a potential
as well, as scientists study the use of euglenophycin

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.390
to fight cancer.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:26.670
And unlike nematodes, which we decidedly do
not want to ingest, there is some culinary

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.560
promise for Euglena.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.420
Like Euglena gracilis, which for all of its
small size, manages to pack in large amounts

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.060
of proteins and vitamins.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:43.700
There’s an underlying question in this video
about all these microbes we’ve seen: are

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:44.840
they good?

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.060
Or bad?

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:47.220
Or neither?

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:49.740
The answer, of course, varies.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:56.500
We’re still grasping the extent of what
it means to share a world with organisms that

00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:02.389
we weren’t able to even see until a few
centuries ago, dealing not only with the consequences

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:07.750
of their existence but also of our own response
to what we’ve learned.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:12.780
Understanding bacteria and other microbes,
for example, that helped us usher in the modern

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.330
era of antibiotics, which has saved so many
lives.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:23.410
But antibiotic overuse has also led to the
growing threat of antibiotic-resistant bacteria.

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:29.980
We can’t not try to fight disease or find
uses for microbes, just as we can’t not

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.360
interact with the world around us.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:38.889
The challenge then of making use of our knowledge
responsibly, of categorizing good and bad—whether

00:08:38.889 --> 00:08:49.240
at the scale of a microbe or much, much larger—hinges
on our ability to identify our place among

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:56.240
all of this, to better understand the complexities
of the good and the bad of our own actions.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:06.680
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:14.460
And thank you of course to all of the people who support this channel at patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:17.680
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:20.180
check our Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:25.380
And if you want to subscribe to this channel. You should. Do that.

